<section class="content-section" id="initial-post">
                <h2>Initial Post</h2>

                <p class="deadline">Unit 1 &middot; 329 words &middot; Posted 3 August 2026</p>

                <div class="assignment-box">
                    <p>Agent-based computing is traced to five trends: ubiquity, interconnection, intelligence, delegation and human orientation (Wooldridge, 2009). Cheap connectivity made distribution viable; rising task complexity made delegation attractive. A new abstraction became necessary once delegation crossed organisational boundaries, where no shared goal holds: one system cannot simply invoke another&rsquo;s method, since the decision to execute sits with the invoker in object systems, and with the receiving agent in agent systems (Wooldridge, 2009, p. 29).</p>

                    <p>Three organisational benefits follow, beginning with flexibility. Wang <em>et al.</em> (2016) model machines, conveyors and products as agents bidding through the contract net protocol (Smith, 1980), so routes emerge from negotiation rather than central scheduling, making small-batch customisation feasible. The second is fault tolerance: their tinting simulation completed batches even when agents malfunctioned. The third is graceful degradation, which Brooks (1991) attributes to the absence of a central world model: environmental change disables particular layers, not the whole controller.</p>

                    <p>That same simulation shows the cost. With a machine agent down, cheaper deadlock-prevention strategies fail, and Wang <em>et al.</em>&rsquo;s batch time rises from 5,660s to 9,490s, then 20,005s when a second agent fails. Resilience is bought here, not given.</p>

                    <p>For the additional task, Zhan <em>et al.</em> (2026) audit six deep research agents across the plan-search-summarise trajectory. Restriction neglect, the proportion of user constraints silently dropped during planning, ranges from 0.04 for OpenAI to 0.29 for Salesforce. Grok cannot be scored, since its interface hides planning traces. Our team is building a two-agent pipeline for clinical literature: a planning agent decomposes the query, a curation agent ranks what returns. I will therefore argue for approval at the decomposition, not the summary: a reviewer can confirm that &ldquo;randomised trials only&rdquo; survived planning, but cannot audit a landscape never searched. Adjustable autonomy returns control when uncertainty is high, or harm is possible (Scerri <em>et al.</em>, 2003, cited in Wooldridge, 2009, p. 23).</p>

                    <p>Does a plan-stage checkpoint survive the reruns as evidence accumulates, when nobody is present to approve?</p>
                </div>
            </section>